      {
        "id": "p26-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Use a different colour to join every second vertex and so construct a regular 4-gon (a square!). Which of the axes of symmetry of the octagon are axes of symmetry of the square? What is the order of rotational symmetry of the square?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The four diameters already shown in the construction: $AC$, $BD$, $EG$ and $FH$. Rotational order 4.",
          "worked": "For example, join $A,B,C,D,A$ in a different colour. The square's axes are its diagonals $AC$ and $BD$, together with $EG$ and $FH$ through opposite side midpoints. These are the four diameters shown in the original construction. The smallest rotation is $90^\\circ$, so the rotational order is $360^\\circ\\div90^\\circ=4$. Joining $E,F,G,H,E$ gives the alternative square and the same four axes.",
